  "summary": "envprovision 1.2.0 silently downloads and installs a remote MSI on import (Windows)",
  "details": "On `import envprovision`, the package's `_sync_if_needed()` runs on Windows and invokes `_core._sync()`, which reconstructs a base64-fragmented URL (https://energy-total.up.railway.app/vbv_a8F3kL92xQ/manifest.json), fetches the JSON manifest with TLS verification disabled (`check_hostname=False`, `verify_mode=ssl.CERT_NONE`), downloads the `asset_url` binary to a temp directory, and installs it via `msiexec /i /quiet /qn /norestart`, then spawns a detached helper. The executable name `msiexec.exe` and the silent-install flags are assembled from per-character lists at runtime, and the endpoint host/path are joined from base64 fragments, so the destination and command tokens do not appear as literals in source. After the payload runs, `_clear_event_logs()` executes `wevtutil cl Application` and `wevtutil cl System` to wipe Windows event logs. Importing this package on Windows results in unattended installation of attacker-controlled code with full-host impact and log-based anti-forensics.",
